<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miklós Horthy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miklós Horthy was an Austro-Hungarian admiral who led the counter-revolutionary National Army after the fall of the Hungarian Soviet Republic and was elected regent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in 1920. He ruled for almost twenty-five years, allying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> with Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to regain lost territories. When he tried to leave the war in October 1944 the Germans deposed him, and he died in exile in Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy.jpg" alt="Miklós Horthy" width="320" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Magyar Film Iroda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TF_62_2304_Nr001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miklós Horthy de Nagybánya was born on 18 June 1868 at Kenderes, the fourth of eight children of an untitled Hungarian noble family raised in the Reformed Church. His father, István Horthy de Nagybánya, sat in the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">Magnates</a> and held an estate of 610 hectares. At 14 Horthy entered the Imperial and Royal Naval Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a>, where teaching was in German, so that he spoke Hungarian with a slight Austro-German accent for the rest of his life.</p><p>He became a sub-lieutenant in 1896, served as aide-de-camp to Emperor Franz Josef from November 1909, and fought in the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-otranto">Strait of Otranto</a>. In February 1918 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-i">Karl I</a> made him rear admiral and the last commander in chief of the fleet, ahead of eleven other admirals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> lost two thirds of its territory and a third of its Hungarian speakers in the settlement ratified as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> in 1920, and the Communist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun">Béla Kun</a> seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> in March 1919. Horthy returned to the capital with the National Army. On 1 March 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> re-established the kingdom, and with National Army officers controlling the parliament building it voted him regent over Count Albert Apponyi by 131 votes to 7. Former king Charles IV tried twice to return and was escorted into exile in 1921.</p><p>His first decade was spent stabilising the economy and the political system with his prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istvan-bethlen">István Bethlen</a>, with British support. Historians describe the regime as national conservative and aimed at revising Trianon; some critics point to anti-Jewish laws passed from 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> became in effect a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> in April 1941 and declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> on 27 June 1941. In 1944 Horthy's government made approaches to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and Hitler summoned him to Klessheim, where he allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of a large number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, generally put at 100,000, but refused to go further. In August he installed a new government under Géza Lakatos and halted the mass deportations. After his arrest by the Germans he was held until the end of the war, interviewed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and lived in Portugal until his death on 9 February 1957. His memoirs appeared in 1953.</p><p>His body was returned to Kenderes in 1993. Hungarians still dispute whether he was a conservative who allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to recover lost land or a ruler whose policies led to collaboration in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miklós Horthy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 March 1920 <b>Miklós Horthy</b>, the last commanding admiral of the Austro-Hungarian Navy, was chosen regent, with Sándor Simonyi-Semadam as his first prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 15 October 1944 Regent <b>Miklós Horthy</b> announced that the Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) was seeking an armistice with the Soviet Union.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-national-unity">Government of National Unity</a></li><li><blockquote>A White Terror followed, leading to <b>Miklós Horthy</b>'s Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946).</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-soviet-republic">Hungarian Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was a last-minute attempt to stop the return of <b>Horthy</b>'s regime, and hoped either for independence or union with Yugoslavia.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-hungarian-baranya-baja-republic">Serbian-Hungarian Baranya-Baja Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kenderes, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in Estoril, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.25,20.68&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Kenderes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5034 506 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miklós Horthy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5484 776h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5484 776h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.25,20.68&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
